node=dict(id='history_of_emotions',label='History of emotions',date_label='Earlier programme 1941 · Reddy’s framework 2001 · Rosenwein’s essay 2002 and book 2006 · methods discussion 2010',layer='historiographical_developments',period='period_4',hunt_core_paradigm=False,entry_kind='group',entry_type='Research field',description='Emotions become objects and resources of historical inquiry: their valuations, expression and relation to action require evidence rather than an assumption of timeless feeling. Reddy’s emotives and emotional regimes connect expression, experience and enforced norms. Rosenwein’s emotional communities emphasize overlapping groups and movement between them. Their disagreement about political scale remains visible. Earlier programmes, including Febvre’s 1941 call, are resources to examine critically; the selected later works neither found nor delimit the whole field.',representative_figures_and_works='Earlier programme: Lucien Febvre, “La sensibilité et l’histoire” (1941; reprinted in Combats pour l’histoire, 1953). Selected frameworks: William M. Reddy, The Navigation of Feeling: A Framework for the History of Emotions (2001); Barbara H. Rosenwein, “Worrying about Emotions in History” (2002), Emotional Communities in the Early Middle Ages (2006; paperback 2007). Named interlocutor: Norbert Elias, The Civilizing Process (1939), through Rosenwein’s 2002 criticism. Retrospective discussion: Jan Plamper’s interview with Reddy, Rosenwein and Peter Stearns (2010); only the specified Reddy/Rosenwein answers were read here.',source_ids=[FEB,REDDY,ROS,IDENTITY,INTERVIEW],scope_note='A bounded field entry distinguishing selected approaches, not a unified school or a census of its practitioners. Period 4 places the selected 2001–2010 interventions for teaching; the 1941 programme is an earlier root, not a founding date. Primary reading: Reddy’s 2001 publisher preface ix–xiii; Rosenwein’s 2002 essay, 821–822, 826–828 and 842–845; selected own answers in Plamper’s 2010 interview, Reddy 237–243 and Rosenwein 255–256. Existing Febvre reading qualifications remain. Rosenwein’s 2006 book is bibliographically checked but unread. The entry keeps political norms, overlapping communities and the interpretation of representations distinct. Later emotional-practice approaches, the full Stearns emotionology programme, sensory history and Sara Ahmed’s related affect inquiry require separate research; they are not silently equated with these frameworks. Review coverage retains the ordinary 2000 baseline; the selected 2001–2010 interventions do not extend it. No comprehensive review of later or non-European traditions is asserted. Dissertation counts guided discovery only and establish no relationship, agreement or field origin.',representative_people=roster,strands=strands,coverage=dict(reviewed_through=2000,basis='Retains the atlas’s ordinary reviewed-through-2000 baseline. The 2001–2010 events are selected interventions, an edition and a reception witness, not a systematic survey of the later field or an extension of review-through coverage. The selected earlier programme is not a field foundation or endpoint.'),chronology=dict(version=1,label='Earlier programme and selected methods, 1941–2010',selection_note='Dates identify selected publications, a paperback edition and a retrospective discussion. They do not define a field origin, continuous duration or end.',events=events,intervals=[],continuation=dict(status='not_asserted',basis='No continuous historical interval or cessation is established by this bounded selection.'),interpretation='The 1941 item is an earlier programme; 2001, 2002 and 2006 identify distinct selected works. The 2007 paperback is an edition, and 2010 records methodological discussion. No sole founder, founding date or end is inferred.',prepared_on=DAY,editorial_record='data/production-batches/person-entries-1.166/acceptance.json'))
